  "knowledgePattern": "Align a weighted geometric tail with a scaled copy",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Compare a scaled copy",
      "body": "<p>Write several terms of the tail and of one half of it, lining up equal powers in the denominators.</p>",
      "recap": "Subtracting aligned algebraic expressions can cancel a repeated part while leaving a simpler remainder.",
      "pitfall": "The original sequence is not a GP: its numerators also change.",
      "pause": "Subtract the two expressions and identify what remains.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l01",
          "reminder": "Subtracting aligned algebraic expressions can cancel a repeated part while leaving a simpler remainder."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sum the simpler tail",
      "body": "<p>Separate any unmatched first term from the repeating-ratio series produced by your subtraction.</p>",
      "recap": "An infinite GP has sum first term divided by one minus its ratio when the absolute ratio is below one.",
      "pitfall": "Keep the starting index and the scale factor on the original tail.",
      "pause": "Sum the remainder and solve for the requested tail.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l09",
          "reminder": "An infinite GP has sum first term divided by one minus its ratio when the absolute ratio is below one."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo></mrow></math> and halve this expression. Align equal denominators before subtracting.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo></mrow></math>. The remaining tail is a GP with first term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, whose sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, answer D. This is not the ordinary GP formula applied to the original terms, whose ratios vary.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The subtraction is legitimate: from index 2 onwards, successive positive terms have ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, so the tail is bounded by a convergent GP. As a check, the formula gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and tends to zero.</p>"
}
